{
  "id": "SEC-01-questionnaire-flow",
  "order": 1,
  "title": "Questionnaire flow overview",
  "purpose": "Define the steps, their order and the conditions for showing each one. Also define the end of the questionnaire: permit reminder, discarding answers that no longer apply, and read-only after submission.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "id": "US-01.1",
      "title": "Show the source tax step only to employees who are subject to source tax",
      "as_a": "hr",
      "i_want": "the source tax questions (denomination, spouse employment, children, cross-border questions) to be shown only to employees who are subject to source tax",
      "so_that": "I no longer have to work out up front who must answer them, and employees who are not subject to source tax are not asked for personal data no party needs",
      "priority": "P1",
      "priority_reason": "HR states it never knows up front who has to answer the source tax questions; asking everyone would collect data that must not be collected (G-01).",
      "acceptance_criteria": [
        {
          "id": "AC-01.1.1",
          "title": "Who sees the source tax questions",
          "given": "the employee has answered country of residence and, where asked, nationality, C permit, civil status and the spouse questions",
          "when": "the questionnaire decides whether to show the source tax step",
          "then": "the source tax step is shown only if the employee is subject to source tax: everyone living abroad, whatever their nationality; and anyone living in Switzerland who is not Swiss, has no C permit and is not married or in a registered partnership with a Swiss national or C permit holder who lives in Switzerland. Anyone with Swiss citizenship counts as Swiss. Registered partnership counts as married and separated as single. Assumption, to be verified by a domain expert: HR's consolidated rule is applied as stated, and 'here' in HR's wording means Switzerland (D-043). The civil status mapping is also an assumption (D-003).",
